WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.970
- I will say, I have sat down

00:00:01.970 --> 00:00:03.270
personally with Mark Zuckerberg,

00:00:03.270 --> 00:00:04.980
I talk about this in the book.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.050
He came to see me back
in September of 2019,

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:08.970
I believe it was.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:12.000
I found that meeting much more interesting

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.840
because that was a face-to-face meeting

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.220
which he was really trying to
change my view about Facebook.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:17.580
I mean, I think that's
why he came to see me.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:20.060
This was in Washington,
in my senate office

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:22.690
and he came to see me and he
really tried to convince me

00:00:22.690 --> 00:00:25.510
that Facebook should be
kind of a national champion,

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:28.100
that we needed Facebook
to be big and powerful

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.690
in order to take on Communist China.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:32.550
Then he really went on and on about this

00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:33.383
for quite some time.

00:00:33.383 --> 00:00:37.280
When I turned the subject
to the question of monopoly,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.406
he got much less
interested in conversation.

00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:42.440
He said "Well, we're not
a monopoly, we're not."

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:43.967
I said, "Okay, well, if you're not,

00:00:43.967 --> 00:00:45.600
and if you're not afraid of competition,

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.110
why don't you sell off
Instagram and WhatsApp?

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:49.380
I mean, you bought them

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.073
so that they wouldn't compete with you.

00:00:51.073 --> 00:00:52.730
You didn't start them, you bought them.

00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:53.642
Why don't you sell you?

00:00:53.642 --> 00:00:55.490
Why don't you spin them off?"

00:00:55.490 --> 00:00:58.740
And that made him very angry,
he was very offended by that.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:01.490
He thought that that was
a ridiculous suggestion.

00:01:01.490 --> 00:01:02.323
And I said, "Well,

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:03.470
that's 'cause you're
committed to your monopoly.

00:01:03.470 --> 00:01:05.200
I mean, you don't want competition.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.180
You wanna be able to
control the whole show,"

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:09.827
and that's really the
problem with these companies.

00:01:09.827 --> 00:01:12.410
(upbeat music)

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.040
- I'm Dave Rubin and joining
me today is the junior senator

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.000
from the great State of Missouri,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.877
and the author of the new book,

00:01:23.877 --> 00:01:27.310
"The Tyranny of Big Tech"
Senator Josh Hawley,

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:29.010
welcome to the Rubin Report.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:30.340
- Thanks for having me.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.880
- Senator, what are the
chances that this video

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.390
is not gonna be algorithmically
depressed by YouTube?

00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:39.933
Do we have any chance that
anyone's gonna see this thing?

00:01:39.933 --> 00:01:42.870
- I don't no. Well, given
my history with big tech,

00:01:42.870 --> 00:01:45.750
maybe not, but we'll
keep our fingers crossed.

00:01:45.750 --> 00:01:48.060
- I've got a similar
history with big tech.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:50.680
So I wanna start by, I just literally

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.000
in the last five minutes,
as we sat down with you,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.680
I saw two headlines that I thought,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:57.640
okay, these are perfect
to tee up for the Senator.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.910
So Breitbart is just reporting
in the last couple of minutes

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:03.360
that I'll read you the exact tweet.

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:06.080
Twitter is reportedly developing a feature

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:09.360
to make users reconsider sending messages

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:13.173
that its system detects as quote mean.

00:02:15.615 --> 00:02:18.015
I suspect that will include
a lot of your stuff.

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:21.370
- Well, I suspect what will
happen is, well, first of all

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:22.203
if they actually enforced that,

00:02:22.203 --> 00:02:24.607
wouldn't that shut down all of Twitter?

00:02:24.607 --> 00:02:25.440
- That is for Twitter.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:27.890
- I thought Twitter seems to subsist on

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:31.130
but of course I in practice,
I'm sure that will mean

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:32.780
that to conservatives and libertarians

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.020
that their tweets are all silenced

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:37.370
and the left can can go
on with the hate mongering

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.330
as long as they like,
that's usually how it works.

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.280
- Yeah. That pretty much is it.

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.220
And then this other one,
which it seems obvious

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:46.600
and I actually warned that
this was gonna be coming.

00:02:46.600 --> 00:02:49.320
Twitter is now, so this
is from Becker News,

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.210
Twitter is now suspending users

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.250
who share Donald Trump's statements.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.680
So as you know, Trump
launched ineffective blog,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:57.750
it wasn't sort of people were
saying he was gonna come out

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:00.110
with this big platform,
apparently that's not it,

00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:01.840
but he launched this
thing with just buttons

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:03.940
that could send stuff
to Twitter and Facebook.

00:03:03.940 --> 00:03:06.440
Now they're actually deleting accounts

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:09.143
that just link to his stuff.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:11.833
- Yeah, this is unbelievable

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:16.950
and their determination to
completely silence and erase

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.965
and cancel the former
president of the United States.

00:03:20.965 --> 00:03:21.798
It's just shocking.

00:03:21.798 --> 00:03:25.230
And the fact that they have
the power, the monopoly power

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.370
to do this and to get by with it

00:03:27.370 --> 00:03:29.651
and that there's nowhere else
the former president can go.

00:03:29.651 --> 00:03:31.300
And think about this former
president United States

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.310
had what, 78, 80 million
followers or something on Twitter

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.410
and many more on the other platforms,

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.680
and there's no alternative for him to go.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:42.410
He's gotta try and create his own thing.

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:43.710
I mean, that tells you something

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.800
about the monopoly power
of these companies.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:47.270
- So let's start right at the heart of it

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.940
because you are a
constitutional conservative

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:51.310
and at least as far as I know,

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:52.870
we're still governed by the constitution,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:55.260
although I'm not quite sure these days,

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.297
but I know that a lot of
people, every time you say,

00:03:58.297 --> 00:04:00.710
"Okay, we've got to do
something about big tech,"

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:01.980
they say, "Well, what do you mean?

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.230
They're private companies

00:04:03.230 --> 00:04:05.160
and we should just leave them alone."

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.323
What is your argument against that?

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.960
- Well, listen, a monopoly is
a threat to the free market

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.640
and also a threat to liberty.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:14.370
I mean, this is the
problem for those of us

00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:16.560
who believe in free market capitalism

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.480
is that monopoly stifles competition.

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:24.480
Monopoly raises barriers to
new entrance into the market,

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.360
slows down innovation

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:28.630
and of course gives the
holders of the monopoly

00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:30.610
incredible power in politics

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:33.480
and we're seeing that now
with these big tech companies.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:34.710
So I would just say

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:36.047
to those who consider themselves

00:04:36.047 --> 00:04:37.780
are defenders of the free market,

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:40.510
to those who considered themselves
defenders of capitalism,

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:43.960
that it is incumbent upon
us to dismantle monopolies

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.470
so that the market could function,

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.880
we can have more competition,

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:48.610
consumers could have more choice.

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:52.420
Bottom line politically
is our framers understood

00:04:52.420 --> 00:04:55.270
monopoly and liberty do not go together

00:04:55.270 --> 00:04:57.070
and we've gotta recover
that understanding.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.780
- Do you think, in some sense though

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:01.480
the ship has just kinda sailed here.

00:05:01.480 --> 00:05:02.960
I covered all the hearings,

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:05.730
obviously, you were very
involved in all of those.

00:05:05.730 --> 00:05:08.240
And when Trump was in
power, there was this idea

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.420
that maybe something's gonna happen.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.552
But at this point Biden's in charge,

00:05:12.552 --> 00:05:14.540
the Democrats are in charge and it's like,

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:17.140
well, why would they do
anything on this stuff

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:19.160
because it's helping them?

00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:19.993
- Yeah, there's no doubt.

00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:22.110
I think the Democrats
have really fallen in love

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.000
with the monopoly power
of these companies.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.040
And if you listen to those hearings

00:05:26.040 --> 00:05:29.120
and cover them as you did,
what do you hear over and over

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.270
is the Democrats saying the
companies out of censor more,

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.860
they gotta use their
power more aggressively.

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:34.770
I mean, they're no longer worried

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.050
about the concentration of power.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:38.090
They're basically
cheering the companies on

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:40.280
and then threatening to regulate them

00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.280
if the companies don't get more censorious

00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:43.940
which is sort of hard to imagine.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:48.360
But I do think that the
Democrats, I have really given up

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.830
on the monopoly question
because they've come to love it

00:05:50.830 --> 00:05:52.670
but that doesn't mean that
the conservatives have to.

00:05:52.670 --> 00:05:54.810
I mean, we have to now more than ever

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.840
get back to their founders understanding

00:05:56.840 --> 00:05:59.700
that monopoly and liberty
are not compatible

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.210
and what we need is more competition.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.470
What we need is to
return power and control

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:05.770
to the American people,

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:07.840
that includes control
over your own speech,

00:06:07.840 --> 00:06:09.840
control over your own
personal information,

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:11.000
your personal data.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:13.650
And I think we've gotta be
bold and pushing forward

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.210
reforms and legislative
proposals that will do that.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:18.307
- Why do you think Trump
didn't move on this?

00:06:18.307 --> 00:06:20.430
There was this threat of doing something

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:22.307
about Section 230 of
the Communications Act

00:06:22.307 --> 00:06:25.020
that we've covered a million times before,

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:26.030
but nothing happened.

00:06:26.030 --> 00:06:28.770
What do you think was the hold up there?

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.580
- I think that really the White House

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:33.360
didn't get focused on it
and the president, I think,

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:35.458
did not begin to really push it

00:06:35.458 --> 00:06:36.790
until it was in an election season

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:38.730
and it was very hard then at that point

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:40.240
to galvanize Congress into action.

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.220
But also the real sticking
point was never the White House,

00:06:43.220 --> 00:06:44.280
it was always Congress.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.410
And a lot of listen, when
I came to the Senate,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:50.270
just two years ago, and I
began proposing a to reform

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:54.160
overhaul Section 230 to
break up these monopolies,

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:56.690
I couldn't find very many
takers on my side of the aisle.

00:06:56.690 --> 00:06:59.430
I mean, there were very,
very few Republicans

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:02.390
who wanted to be associated
with that or involved with that.

00:07:02.390 --> 00:07:04.770
And so I think you just didn't
have a lot of enthusiasm

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:06.660
and that's 'cause
Republican elected officials

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.320
were a lot of the times out
of step with their own voters.

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.297
Their own voters were saying,

00:07:10.297 --> 00:07:14.220
"Wow, these companies have too much power.

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:16.040
They are radical left.

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.700
They are censoring speech.

00:07:17.700 --> 00:07:20.990
They are shutting down our
speech in the democratic process,

00:07:20.990 --> 00:07:22.020
interfering with it."

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:23.970
But you said Republicans who
weren't willing to move on it.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.400
I think that that is changing.

00:07:25.400 --> 00:07:28.100
But as you say, the last two, four years

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:29.590
were really a missed opportunity

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.340
for Republicans in that respect

00:07:31.340 --> 00:07:33.050
and now we've got to
make up for lost time.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:36.190
And we can do that by being
bold with our thinking,

00:07:36.190 --> 00:07:39.310
bold in our proposals, and
to go to the American people

00:07:39.310 --> 00:07:42.860
with an agenda to trust bust
here in the 21st century.

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.850
- But do you fear that now that
the Democrats are in charge

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:48.670
that if they were to do
anything about all of this

00:07:48.670 --> 00:07:50.130
that it would actually just be worse

00:07:50.130 --> 00:07:52.557
because you made the point,
they were basically saying,

00:07:52.557 --> 00:07:55.390
"Hey, if you guys don't regulate speech,

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:56.280
we're gonna punish you."

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:59.340
I mean, that's basically
what AOC said to Zuckerberg

00:07:59.340 --> 00:08:01.940
and he pretty much was like,
"Well, I can't do that,

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:03.070
I'm not God."

00:08:03.070 --> 00:08:05.570
But now that they have the power like,

00:08:05.570 --> 00:08:07.320
it's hard for me to envision basically

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:10.850
them using that power to expand freedom.

00:08:10.850 --> 00:08:12.438
- Yes. I agree with you.

00:08:12.438 --> 00:08:13.480
And I think this is one of the reasons

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:15.000
why I'm very, very skeptical

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:17.630
of increasing government
regulation over the companies,

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:19.680
especially the way the Democrats want to.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:23.740
Democrats wanna further
regulate the speech content

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:27.500
that's on the web and
that's on these platforms.

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:28.950
I mean, that's what they really wanna do

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.730
and they wanna tell the companies

00:08:30.730 --> 00:08:33.390
that you need to define this as offensive

00:08:33.390 --> 00:08:35.250
and you need to find that as threatening

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.900
and you need to take down this.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:38.980
That I think is exactly
the wrong way to go.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:41.330
We don't need less speech,
we need more speech.

00:08:41.330 --> 00:08:43.650
We don't need more political control,

00:08:43.650 --> 00:08:46.930
we need less political
control and more freedom

00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:48.340
on the internet and everywhere else.

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:50.330
So I think that the way to go

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:52.360
is not to increase the
government involvement

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:53.470
but to break the companies up.

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:55.300
I mean, let's get the free
market involved again.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.820
Let's get some competitors in that space.

00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.790
Another thing I would say is

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:01.030
let's get people the right
to sue these companies,

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.290
these monopolies, if they get deplatformed

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:06.320
and they're treated in bad faith.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:08.380
It used to be, I mean, it still is true

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:11.620
in every other walk of life
of every other industry

00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:14.160
that if a company violates
its contract with you,

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.100
you can sue them.

00:09:15.100 --> 00:09:16.150
Well, with big tech,

00:09:16.150 --> 00:09:19.160
you can't sue them if they
violate their terms of agreement

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:21.000
because the federal
government protects them,

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.900
immunizes them, that's Section 230.

00:09:22.900 --> 00:09:26.050
I think we ought to withdrawal
that and allow people to sue

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.340
if they're treated in bad
faith by these companies,

00:09:29.340 --> 00:09:31.270
if these companies violate
their terms of service,

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.500
I think that could be a game changer.

00:09:32.500 --> 00:09:35.190
- Can you talk about what
actually breaking them up

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:36.023
would look like?

00:09:36.023 --> 00:09:39.070
'Cause I've seen a lot of
my more libertarian friends

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:40.810
basically say that is the answer.

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:42.530
Like they've just sort of hit the end,

00:09:42.530 --> 00:09:43.760
they got to the end of the road

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:44.593
and it's like, all right,

00:09:44.593 --> 00:09:46.470
we don't really like to do this stuff

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.700
but I think they're kinda with you,

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:48.960
it's like we have no choice.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:50.160
What does that actually mean?

00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:51.650
So we're gonna break up Twitter,

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:53.750
like what does that really look like?

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:55.080
- Well, here's where I would start.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.220
I would start by making
these companies spin off

00:09:58.220 --> 00:09:59.660
all of the various industries

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:02.740
that they've tried to
consolidate under their control.

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.920
So for example, Amazon.

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:08.050
Amazon has the dominant
e-commerce platform in the world,

00:10:08.050 --> 00:10:09.110
we're all familiar with that.

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.200
It also though has a major major share

00:10:11.200 --> 00:10:14.960
if not a controlling share
in the cloud, that's AWS.

00:10:14.960 --> 00:10:17.090
It also has its own
retail line of products

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:18.790
that it sells on its own platform,

00:10:18.790 --> 00:10:20.670
using information from competitors.

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:22.860
They shouldn't be able to do
all of these things at once.

00:10:22.860 --> 00:10:24.540
I mean, this is really
what used to be called

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:25.750
a conglomerate merger,

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.310
where you have this massive
conglomeration of industries

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:32.510
under one set of hands under one control.

00:10:32.510 --> 00:10:35.410
I think we oughta just make
them spin those companies off

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:38.170
by just saying in law
that they can't combine

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.880
these different industries
into one giant tech company.

00:10:40.880 --> 00:10:42.590
Almost all these tech companies do it.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:44.550
I mean, Google does the same thing.

00:10:44.550 --> 00:10:47.090
Google has the Search, of course,

00:10:47.090 --> 00:10:49.100
the dominant search platform in the world.

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.190
But Google also owns the advertising,

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:53.810
the digital advertising
sphere at a monopoly level

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:56.170
such that if you want to advertise
anything in the internet,

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:57.910
you've gotta go through Google.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.310
We should make them spin
off those businesses,

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:03.150
sell them off, Facebook,
WhatsApp, Instagram,

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:04.720
they should have to sell those off.

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:06.720
So that when I think about
breaking up big tech,

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:07.940
that's where I would start.

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:11.020
Let's reverse this conglomerate mergers

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.070
that big tech has done and performed

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.270
by gobbling up all of
these other companies

00:11:15.270 --> 00:11:17.380
and let's begin to
reintroduce some competition.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:18.330
- So you don't think we can send

00:11:18.330 --> 00:11:21.260
a couple of government bureaucrats
into the Google offices

00:11:21.260 --> 00:11:23.240
to look at the algorithm
and fix that thing.

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:24.590
Is that what you're saying?

00:11:25.460 --> 00:11:29.460
- You know, I think that
that's the kind of expertise.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:31.390
I don't trust the federal
government to do much of anything.

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.090
I certainly don't trust them

00:11:33.090 --> 00:11:35.170
to try and fine tune these companies.

00:11:35.170 --> 00:11:37.870
And I would say that what
that really amounts to

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.190
to get serious about it,

00:11:39.190 --> 00:11:41.240
is that really amounts
to a form of corporatism.

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:42.827
That's when the government
says to big business,

00:11:42.827 --> 00:11:44.940
"You know what, why don't we partner up

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:47.060
and we'll work hand in glove
and we'll work together."

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:48.660
That's what the Democrats want I think.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.710
The Democrats have become cooperators.

00:11:50.710 --> 00:11:52.020
They wanna see this alliance

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.550
between big business and big government,

00:11:54.550 --> 00:11:56.330
they're pursuing in
building that alliance.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:58.050
I think as conservatives
we should say, "No, no, no.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:11:59.240
We reject that whole premise.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.350
We don't want big business
or big government,

00:12:01.350 --> 00:12:04.460
we don't want them working
together, we want competition.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.800
We want there to be liberty

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:08.110
and we're gonna pursue
policies that will get that.

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:10.270
- Can you talk a little
bit about what it's like

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.000
to be in those meetings?

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:13.190
It's like when you're sitting there

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:14.870
and you're looking at Jack from Twitter,

00:12:14.870 --> 00:12:17.760
who looks like he just got off
a three week ayahuasca trip

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.050
and you got Zuckerberg who looks
like he was just pulled off

00:12:21.050 --> 00:12:25.440
like a synthetic human, like whatever,

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.660
like what do you make of these guys?

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:28.493
Like really?

00:12:28.493 --> 00:12:30.140
Like, what is that experience like?

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:32.060
Also knowing that the entire world

00:12:32.060 --> 00:12:33.370
is watching everything they say,

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:34.430
watching everything you say,

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:37.130
it's all gonna be cut
and edited in crazy ways

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:38.670
and everything else.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:40.400
- I've actually found
that their appearances

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:42.430
before the committees, for my cases,

00:12:42.430 --> 00:12:44.410
the judiciary committee
that they've appeared before

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.550
a number of times tend
to be very, shall we say,

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:47.890
unenlightening.

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:49.550
They don't, and you know this, covered it,

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:51.760
commented on it, analyze it,

00:12:51.760 --> 00:12:54.300
they say nothing over
and over and over again,

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:55.450
and they try to say nothing

00:12:55.450 --> 00:12:57.320
in new and more interesting ways.

00:12:57.320 --> 00:13:00.430
And I personally think that those hearings

00:13:00.430 --> 00:13:03.200
have a value in putting questions to them

00:13:03.200 --> 00:13:05.800
and exposing the fact
that they prevaricate

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:08.420
and obviously and sometimes
just outright lie,

00:13:08.420 --> 00:13:09.370
that's the value

00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:11.930
but in terms of actually
extracting information,

00:13:11.930 --> 00:13:14.410
they really, they don't give you anything.

00:13:14.410 --> 00:13:15.680
I mean, there's really little value there.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:18.890
I will say, I have sat down
personally with Mark Zuckerberg,

00:13:18.890 --> 00:13:20.610
I talk about this in the book,

00:13:20.610 --> 00:13:23.700
he came to see me in back
in September of 2019,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:24.600
I believe it was.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.610
I found that meeting much more interesting

00:13:27.610 --> 00:13:29.470
because that was a face-to-face meeting

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:31.493
which he was really trying to
change my view about Facebook.

00:13:31.493 --> 00:13:33.220
I mean, I think that's
why he came to see me.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.700
This was in Washington in my senate office

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.320
and he came to see me and he
really tried to convince me

00:13:38.320 --> 00:13:41.140
that Facebook should be
kind of a national champion.

00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:43.730
That we needed Facebook
to be big and powerful

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:46.320
in order to take on Communist China.

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:48.970
Then he really went on and on
about this for quite some time

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:52.900
when I turned the subject
to the question of monopoly

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:54.550
he got much less interested
in the conversation.

00:13:54.550 --> 00:13:58.090
And he said, "Well, I mean,
we're not a monopoly. We don't."

00:13:58.090 --> 00:13:59.850
I said, "Okay, well, if you're not,

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:01.230
if you're not afraid of competition

00:14:01.230 --> 00:14:03.730
why don't you sell off
Instagram and WhatsApp?

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.010
I mean, you, you, you bought them

00:14:05.010 --> 00:14:06.630
so that they wouldn't compete with you.

00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:08.370
You didn't, you didn't
start them, you bought them.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.920
Why don't you spin them off?"

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:14.370
And that made him very angry,
he was very offended by that.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:17.120
He thought that that was
a ridiculous suggestion.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:19.110
And I said, "Well, 'cause you're
committed to your monopoly.

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:20.980
I mean, you don't want competition.

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:23.810
You wanna be able to
control the whole show."

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:25.580
And that's really the
problem with these companies.

00:14:25.580 --> 00:14:27.420
- Do you have some sympathy for these guys

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:28.740
when they're testifying?

00:14:28.740 --> 00:14:32.287
Like Zuckerberg there, in that
moment when he kinda said,

00:14:32.287 --> 00:14:34.530
"We just can't do all of this stuff."

00:14:34.530 --> 00:14:35.930
That's what he said to AOC.

00:14:35.930 --> 00:14:37.660
I actually kind of
sympathize with the guy,

00:14:37.660 --> 00:14:39.920
I know it's hard to
sympathize with these people,

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:42.270
but at some level they
start these tech companies,

00:14:42.270 --> 00:14:44.810
I don't think they're
all evil necessarily.

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:46.740
I think they get lost in their own thing

00:14:46.740 --> 00:14:50.400
and it's almost an unwinnable
position in a bizarre sense.

00:14:50.400 --> 00:14:52.600
But do you have any sympathy for him?

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:54.460
- You know, I don't tend
to be very sympathetic

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:55.690
when I'm getting lied to.

00:14:55.690 --> 00:15:00.680
And I do think that they have
deliberately misled Congress

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:02.830
many times and would do it and will do it

00:15:02.830 --> 00:15:03.920
if you'd let them get by with it.

00:15:03.920 --> 00:15:04.753
I will say that though,

00:15:04.753 --> 00:15:06.750
sometimes depending on
who's questioning them,

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.480
you do actually feel a
little bit of sympathy.

00:15:08.480 --> 00:15:10.080
And some of the questions
that they have to answer

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:12.370
are really ludicrous and ridiculous.

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:16.020
But on the whole and my view
is these guys are billionaires,

00:15:16.020 --> 00:15:17.770
they're not stupid
people as you point out,

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.670
these are very capable people,

00:15:19.670 --> 00:15:21.910
they didn't get to where
they are by being dumb.

00:15:21.910 --> 00:15:24.910
I mean, they're very smart,
they're very accomplished

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.410
and they can certainly hold their own.

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.140
So I tend not to feel too sorry for them.

00:15:28.140 --> 00:15:30.060
I wish they'd be more forthcoming.

00:15:30.060 --> 00:15:32.100
I wished they'd be a
little bit more honest.

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:33.930
But at the end of the day,

00:15:33.930 --> 00:15:38.120
we can't look to anyone CEO's
personal characteristics

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.850
and personal character
traits and hope for the best.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:42.910
What we've got to do is make decisions

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:44.570
about the structure of our economy.

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.220
And when you have monopolies

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:48.590
who are actively suppressed in competition

00:15:48.590 --> 00:15:51.370
and actively trying to control
speech, that's a problem.

00:15:51.370 --> 00:15:52.510
And it doesn't really matter

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.240
who's in charge of the company.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:55.730
What matters is what they're doing

00:15:55.730 --> 00:15:57.830
as a company and their power

00:15:57.830 --> 00:15:59.620
and we've got to do something about that.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.770
- Yeah. Do you sense
that also some of this is

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.240
that they can use a lot
of linguistic tricks

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:06.010
to get out of all of the legal stuff.

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:10.450
So they'll use phrases like
deboosting or shadow banning

00:16:10.450 --> 00:16:13.930
or unbiasing, all of these
things that they mean something

00:16:13.930 --> 00:16:15.610
but they mean different
things to different people.

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:18.940
So I've had Jack from
Twitter tweet directly at me

00:16:18.940 --> 00:16:21.130
in response to me saying,
"We don't shadow ban."

00:16:21.130 --> 00:16:23.710
Well, it's like, we all
know that you shadow ban,

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:27.470
but he may in his up mind just
have a different definition

00:16:27.470 --> 00:16:28.660
of what shadow ban is.

00:16:28.660 --> 00:16:29.960
So they make up words

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:32.640
so that they're never really
on the hook for anything

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.490
- That's right. This
has happened repeatedly.

00:16:34.490 --> 00:16:37.370
It was interesting in the
hearing, the last hearing,

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:39.240
where these folks came before
the judiciary committee,

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:42.560
was I think in November of
2020, so just a few months ago.

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:45.450
And shortly before that hearing,

00:16:45.450 --> 00:16:49.040
I had had a former employee
of Facebook, a whistleblower,

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:52.070
come to my office and say
that he had information

00:16:52.070 --> 00:16:55.300
he wanted to share about
Facebook's censorship practices.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.480
And I tell some of this story in the book.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:16:59.680
And one of the things he revealed to me

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:00.860
and reveal to my staff

00:17:00.860 --> 00:17:03.670
was the existence of an
internal Facebook platform

00:17:03.670 --> 00:17:06.140
called Centra, which allows the company,

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.140
it's like a souped up
surveillance platform,

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:10.650
and it allows the company
to track their users

00:17:10.650 --> 00:17:13.010
across multiple different platforms,

00:17:13.010 --> 00:17:16.190
including across different
usernames and aliases

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.530
and attract their posts to
track their internet activity,

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:20.460
to track what they're doing
in different countries

00:17:20.460 --> 00:17:21.310
or geolocation.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:22.500
I mean, it's extraordinary.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.600
And he's shared with us screenshots

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:27.500
of this platform in use
with particular individuals.

00:17:27.500 --> 00:17:28.333
That was amazing.

00:17:28.333 --> 00:17:31.280
So I get to the hearing and I
asked Zuckerberg about this.

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:34.430
He was clearly very surprised to be asked

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:36.200
but he initially tried to deny it

00:17:36.200 --> 00:17:38.837
by very carefully choosing
his words and saying,

00:17:38.837 --> 00:17:43.090
"I'm not familiar with any
such tool by that name."

00:17:43.090 --> 00:17:44.954
- Plausible deniability.

00:17:44.954 --> 00:17:45.880
- Yeah. Wait a minute.

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:47.810
I said, "Sir, are you telling
me that that doesn't exist

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:50.350
or you telling me that you
call it something else?

00:17:50.350 --> 00:17:51.460
I mean, what is it you're trying to say?"

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:53.020
Eventually by the way, Facebook admitted

00:17:53.020 --> 00:17:55.700
they do have that tool
and it is called Centra.

00:17:55.700 --> 00:17:56.910
So he was wrong on all counts,

00:17:56.910 --> 00:17:59.450
but he engaged in this
back and forth with me

00:17:59.450 --> 00:18:02.640
where he was desperately
trying to thread this needle,

00:18:02.640 --> 00:18:06.270
did not outright deny it but
it's suggest I was wrong,

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:07.490
and that's what they do all the time.

00:18:07.490 --> 00:18:09.690
- Does anyone go to jail
after any of these things?

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:11.810
I mean, I know not, not Zuckerberg

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:13.650
or Jack Dorsey from Twitter,

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:17.200
but like when they do that
sort of pretty much lie thing,

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:20.080
does anyone ever get punished
for lying to Congress anymore?

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:20.913
Is that a thing?

00:18:20.913 --> 00:18:22.157
- Not really. No, not really.

00:18:22.157 --> 00:18:25.520
You know, I can remember a
time that a Google executive

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:27.620
testified also before
the judiciary committee

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:29.800
and I was asking him about the tracking

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.120
that Google performs
on its Android phones.

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.900
And I said that, even if you turn off

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:39.190
the location services on your
phone, it still tracks you.

00:18:39.190 --> 00:18:40.657
And he initially outright said to me,

00:18:40.657 --> 00:18:42.260
"No, that's not accurate."

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:43.093
I said, "Really?"

00:18:43.093 --> 00:18:45.067
And so we started walking
through it and I said,

00:18:45.067 --> 00:18:48.290
"I thought that even with
location services off,

00:18:48.290 --> 00:18:49.580
the wifi function on your phone

00:18:49.580 --> 00:18:52.430
still scanned for other wifi in the area,

00:18:52.430 --> 00:18:55.140
still made a note of it,
still stored that information

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:56.870
and still communicated it back to Google."

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:58.810
And then he said, "Oh,
well, yeah, that's true."

00:18:58.810 --> 00:19:01.800
I said, "Well, so it's
still tracking you."

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:02.930
At which point he said, "Well,

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.450
I wouldn't use the word tracking."

00:19:04.450 --> 00:19:05.580
I mean, so there you go.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:08.870
I mean, where it's this constant game

00:19:08.870 --> 00:19:10.420
to get them to be honest with you

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:12.340
sometimes about the most basic stuff.

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:15.210
I mean, I've often think
that these tech folks

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:16.430
when you put them on the stand,

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:17.510
if you say, put them under oath,

00:19:17.510 --> 00:19:19.290
if you asked them if the
sun was shining outside,

00:19:19.290 --> 00:19:20.123
they would prevaricate.

00:19:20.123 --> 00:19:22.282
Oh, well, you know, define sun.

00:19:22.282 --> 00:19:24.550
So that's how these hearings go.

00:19:24.550 --> 00:19:26.870
- Do you think some of
your involvement of this,

00:19:26.870 --> 00:19:28.740
is not only because I
know you actually care

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.380
about the constitution and freedom,

00:19:30.380 --> 00:19:33.150
and I wanna talk about lockdowns
and a lot more obviously,

00:19:33.150 --> 00:19:36.620
but also because you're younger
than the average Senator.

00:19:36.620 --> 00:19:38.620
And partly what we have going on here

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:41.380
is a 78 year old president

00:19:41.380 --> 00:19:43.070
and I got, I'm here in California,

00:19:43.070 --> 00:19:45.810
I got like 106 year old
Senator Dianne Feinstein.

00:19:45.810 --> 00:19:48.150
Like we have these old and I'm not even,

00:19:48.150 --> 00:19:49.710
I don't mean that to be ages Pelosi,

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:50.543
we have these old,

00:19:50.543 --> 00:19:53.520
and even Trump with 74
he would be 75 right now,

00:19:53.520 --> 00:19:56.830
if he was president that
baby boomer generation

00:19:56.830 --> 00:19:59.630
is just hanging on to power too long

00:19:59.630 --> 00:20:01.560
and now there's these new industries

00:20:01.560 --> 00:20:04.390
and they don't know how
to react to these things.

00:20:04.390 --> 00:20:06.644
- I do think there is a
generational component to this.

00:20:06.644 --> 00:20:07.940
I think there's no doubt about it.

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:10.720
And I can tell you,
just from my experience

00:20:10.720 --> 00:20:12.820
in questioning on the judiciary committee,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:14.159
there were multiple times

00:20:14.159 --> 00:20:15.690
where I would be asking
a line of questioning

00:20:15.690 --> 00:20:17.580
that this is really not
earth shattering stuff.

00:20:17.580 --> 00:20:19.820
I mean, this is even
about location services

00:20:19.820 --> 00:20:22.610
we were just talking about,
a lot of people know that.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:24.190
But I was asking some of these questions

00:20:24.190 --> 00:20:26.540
and I had older colleagues

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.390
who just aren't familiar
with these technologies.

00:20:28.390 --> 00:20:29.240
They just don't admit it,

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:31.170
probably do have an iPhone or an Android,

00:20:31.170 --> 00:20:33.330
but they don't really use it that much.

00:20:33.330 --> 00:20:34.840
They use it to make calls, that's it,

00:20:34.840 --> 00:20:36.310
their staff does a lot of the other stuff.

00:20:36.310 --> 00:20:40.577
And I had them come up
to me afterwards and say,

00:20:40.577 --> 00:20:43.770
"I've never heard that
before, is that true?

00:20:43.770 --> 00:20:46.440
I wasn't aware that any
of this was going on

00:20:46.440 --> 00:20:48.260
and that's been a pretty common response.

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:49.430
I think that's just generational.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:52.300
I think that they're not as
familiar with this technology,

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:54.750
and that's one of the
reasons why these hearings,

00:20:54.750 --> 00:20:57.730
you've really seen especially,
a couple of years ago,

00:20:57.730 --> 00:21:00.540
you could really see the tech executives

00:21:00.540 --> 00:21:02.820
tie up members of Congress and knots,

00:21:02.820 --> 00:21:06.210
because the initial question
might be pretty doing good

00:21:06.210 --> 00:21:09.010
from a Senator Congressman,
but then the response,

00:21:09.010 --> 00:21:10.510
they just wouldn't know how to respond.

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.730
And the tech CEO or executive

00:21:12.730 --> 00:21:14.850
would give a prevaricate response

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:17.080
and then there was no follow-up,

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.280
there was no way to untangle that web

00:21:19.280 --> 00:21:20.220
that they had just spun.

00:21:20.220 --> 00:21:23.580
So I'm glad that it's more
people get focused on this

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.340
and spend more time
getting prepped for this.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:28.360
I think Congress is getting
better at doing oversight.

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:30.650
But to go back to earlier
point, we could talk all we want

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.120
about oversight, hearings, lots of talk,

00:21:33.120 --> 00:21:34.290
we have to do something.

00:21:34.290 --> 00:21:35.450
We can talk all we want

00:21:35.450 --> 00:21:38.300
but the time has come to
do something about this

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:40.693
and that's partly what this book is about.

00:21:41.660 --> 00:21:44.050
- So we're sort of dancing
around all the topics in the book

00:21:44.050 --> 00:21:45.030
but let's talk about the book.

00:21:45.030 --> 00:21:46.850
So it's called the "Tyranny of Big Tech"

00:21:46.850 --> 00:21:50.140
and ironically the book
almost didn't get published.

00:21:50.140 --> 00:21:52.610
Can you explain what happened there?

00:21:52.610 --> 00:21:53.890
- Yeah, that's right.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:57.120
The book was initially
commissioned by Simon &amp; Schuster,

00:21:57.120 --> 00:21:59.567
one of the two biggest
publishers, I believe I'm right,

00:21:59.567 --> 00:22:00.900
siting in the country.

00:22:00.900 --> 00:22:02.930
And Simon &amp; Schuster
naturally has been purchased

00:22:02.930 --> 00:22:05.170
by the biggest publisher in the country.

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:07.860
So there you have further
industry consolidation

00:22:07.860 --> 00:22:10.560
just like everywhere else,
but Simon &amp; Schuster,

00:22:10.560 --> 00:22:13.420
sign the contract for the
book before it was written,

00:22:13.420 --> 00:22:15.310
sort of commissioned me in
that sense to write the book.

00:22:15.310 --> 00:22:18.030
I wrote it, they saw we're happy with it

00:22:18.030 --> 00:22:21.190
and then in January they said,
"Oh, actually you know what,

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:22.370
we're not gonna publish it.

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:23.410
We don't think you should
be able to publish it.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:24.970
We don't think you
should be able to speak."

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:28.330
And they said that, actually,
they said to me nothing,

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:29.970
they they never reached
out to me personally,

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:31.430
I read this in a press release.

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:32.263
- That's incredible.

00:22:32.263 --> 00:22:34.970
So they never reached any
anyone on your team to say,

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:36.030
I mean, just even legally,

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:37.627
you'd think they'd have to
reach out to your lawyer to say,

00:22:37.627 --> 00:22:40.100
"Hey, we're severing the
contract or something."

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:42.250
- What they did is that they sent,

00:22:42.250 --> 00:22:44.880
I think they made a phone
call, an informal phone call

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:47.247
to the folks, to my agent and said,

00:22:47.247 --> 00:22:49.680
"We're gonna to cancel
this," but they didn't send,

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:50.690
they didn't reach out to me.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:51.660
They didn't send a letter.

00:22:51.660 --> 00:22:52.493
They didn't, they didn't.

00:22:52.493 --> 00:22:53.326
Then they did a press release.

00:22:53.326 --> 00:22:55.140
And he said, "Well, why,
what are you going to say?"

00:22:55.140 --> 00:22:56.510
And it's like, well, we'll
be putting out a statement.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.640
And so it was like, well,
I guess we'll read it.

00:22:58.640 --> 00:23:00.510
I guess we'll read the
statement, which is what I did.

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:03.680
You know, I read their
statement, which directly

00:23:03.680 --> 00:23:04.950
I can't remember the exact words now

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:05.783
but basically said that,

00:23:05.783 --> 00:23:08.600
you know what Hawley helped
incite a riot on Capitol Hill,

00:23:08.600 --> 00:23:09.930
and you get the impression from that

00:23:09.930 --> 00:23:13.740
that I was there at a
criminal riot on the Hill.

00:23:13.740 --> 00:23:17.850
Totally false, 100% false
they knew it was false.

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:20.140
And the whole reason they did
it is because they went along

00:23:20.140 --> 00:23:22.780
with the woke left mob
that wanted to cancel,

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:25.170
it still does, wants to
cancel conservatives,

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.710
wants to cancel Trump supporters,

00:23:26.710 --> 00:23:29.980
wants to cancel anybody
who will not bow down

00:23:29.980 --> 00:23:32.170
to their woke mentality
and their woke agenda.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:34.550
And I wouldn't then, and I won't now.

00:23:34.550 --> 00:23:36.290
And I'm delighted that
the book had founded

00:23:36.290 --> 00:23:37.480
an independent publisher

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.390
who had the guts to publish it,

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:42.780
who believes in free speech,
a pretty basic American value,

00:23:42.780 --> 00:23:44.940
and now people can read for themselves.

00:23:44.940 --> 00:23:46.710
- Let's give Regnery a
